body:
Two implementations of the same linear scan. Both O(n). One is 40x slower.

```c
// A: sum a 4096x4096 matrix row-major
for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)
        total += m[i][j];

// B: same thing, column-major
for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
        total += m[i][j];
```

Identical operation count. Identical complexity class. B is catastrophically slower because every iteration touches a new cache line, and at 4096 ints per row you also blow the TLB.

I'm not saying complexity analysis is useless, obviously it isn't. I'm saying it deliberately throws away the constant factor, and on modern hardware the constant factor is dominated by memory access patterns that the notation cannot see. "O(n) with good locality" and "O(n) with bad locality" are different algorithms in every way that matters to a user.

The thing I'd want taught alongside it: a cache miss to DRAM is on the order of 200-300 cycles. An L1 hit is about 4. You can do an enormous amount of arithmetic in the time it takes to be wrong about where your data is.
